  "content": "# one operation per user intent keeps concerns separated\n\nEach operation should correspond to a single user action. This keeps operations atomic, testable, and replayable:\n\n```\n\u2705 ADD_LINE_ITEM \u2014 adds one line item\n\u2705 UPDATE_RECIPIENT \u2014 changes the recipient\n\u2705 SET_DUE_DATE \u2014 sets the due date\n\n\u274c UPDATE_INVOICE \u2014 changes recipient, adds items, and sets date in one operation\n```\n\nDescriptive operation names (e.g., `ADD_LINE_ITEM`, `UPDATE_RECIPIENT`) communicate intent better than generic names (e.g., `UPDATE_DATA`).\n\nSince [[operation design uses descriptive names and includes examples and errors]], each operation should also define error types and include examples in the document model.\n\n---\n\nTopics:\n- [[reducer patterns]]\n- [[schema design]]",
